  "live_records": {
    "q-3401": {
      "dom_id": "step-q3401",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Calculate $\\int {e}^{4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "To use integration by parts, let's suppose that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{4x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4{e}^{4x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(4{e}^{4x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\int {e}^{4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{4x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4{e}^{4x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{4x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(4{e}^{4x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4\\int {e}^{4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I.$ We remember to add a constant of integration $C$ at the end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={e}^{4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4J \\\\ I & ={e}^{4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4(-{e}^{4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4I) \\\\ I & ={e}^{4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4{e}^{4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-16I \\\\ 17I & ={e}^{4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4{e}^{4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{4x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{17}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
